<section class="cs-section">
  <h2 class="cs-section-title">Deep Dive: Peer & Upward Review</h2>
  <div class="cs-section-body">
    <p>The introduction of peer and upward review meant previous designs couldn't simply be edited. We needed to redesign the to-do page, review cycle listing, and details page. We overcame the lack of exploration time by referencing how competitor softwares handled it.</p>

    <h3 class="cs-subsection-title">Before</h3>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li>Only self and manager review to consider for each row item.</li>
      <li>Chips used incorrectly to display the review type period.</li>
      <li>Review cards not fully utilising available space.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <img src="/assets/test-perf.png" alt="...">
      <figcaption class="cs-caption">Placeholder caption.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 class="cs-subsection-title">After</h3>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li>Needed to show statuses for up to 5 review types + 2 selection periods. Switched from a 2-column to a 2-row layout — Row 1 for essential cycle details, Row 2 for review statuses.</li>
      <li>Introduced a progress bar in the event details page to balance absolute numbers and completion percentages.</li>
      <li>Increased gap between review period and status for better readability.</li>
      <li>New task page tabs: <strong>"My Review"</strong> for reviews and responses related to me, and <strong>"Team Review"</strong> for everything outside of me — subordinates, peers, direct manager.</li>
    </ul>
    <!-- TODO: Add after screenshot -->
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs-section">
  <h2 class="cs-section-title">Reflection</h2>
  <div class="cs-section-body">
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li><strong>Would do differently:</strong> Use a different layout for the My Review and Team Review cards to reduce wasted space.</li>
      <li><strong>Current limitation:</strong> The system still can't cleanly handle employees who miss submission deadlines or extend deadlines for specific individuals — this requires backend manipulation by developers.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
